module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_pin_claim_compares_two_copies_while_a_third_drifts

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data a_pin_claim_compares_two_copies_while_a_third_drifts: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_pin_claim_compares_two_copies_while_a_third_drifts" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a pin claim compares two copies while a third drifts** (a value is forced to exist twice -- once as a declaration consumers read, once as a literal some parser extracts rather than evaluates. An author writes a claim to keep the two honest, and the claim compares the DECLARATION to a THIRD copy written inside the claim itself. The copy that can actually drift is the one nothing reads.)",

    "INVALID STATE: a claim cited in prose as the wall against a specific drift, which stays green when exactly that drift occurs.",

    "WHY IT SURVIVES REVIEW: the claim is not a decoration in the section 4b sense -- it CAN go red, by editing the declaration -- so a reviewer checking whether the claim has an authorable red finds one and stops. The red it has is not the red it is cited for. The question that separates them is not 'can this fail' but 'can it fail FOR THE STATED CAUSE', and only the second is answered by naming the drift and asking which copies the claim actually reads.",

    "SPECIMEN (PR #11485, review 67242). extdeps.google.drive and extdeps.google.sheets each spell an environment variable name twice, because the service config's auth_source is read by extract_string_value, which pulls a string literal out of the node rather than evaluating a reference. Three annotations cited drive_token_env_matches_the_configured_literal, sheets_token_env_matches_the_configured_literal and marker_property_key_matches_the_created_property as what stops the spellings drifting apart. All three compare a data row to a third literal inside the claim; none reads the auth_source literal or the file-property body literal (that field was appProperties at the time of the specimen and is now properties, for a credential-visibility reason recorded in extdeps.google.drive; the claim's shape is unchanged). Editing the transport literal alone leaves all three green, which is the exact failure the annotations describe. The same PR already knew the shape: a sibling claim in review_sheet_transport_pin_witness states outright that the write side is asserted by a human reading two lines and not by execution.",

    "THE DISCRIMINATOR: a claim whose subject appears THREE times -- declaration, unread literal, and the claim's own copy -- where the assertion joins the two that a single edit moves together. A claim comparing two DECLARATIONS with no third copy between them is the sound sibling and is not this class; in the specimen, drive_and_sheets_read_one_credential is that claim.",

    "RUNG: found at 1, mitigatable. The duplicate is watched by a claim that catches one of the two drifts, and the annotation asserting it catches both is what makes the state worse than an unwatched duplicate.",

    "CEILING 4, structurally impossible, and the path is construction rather than a better claim. The duplicate exists only because a config parse extracts a literal instead of evaluating a declaration reference. A parse that resolved the reference would leave one spelling, and a second spelling would have nothing to drift from -- DESIGN section 5's preference for making the state unwritable over checking it afterwards. No claim over the current shape reaches above rung 2, because no fold in the corpus can read a service config's literals.",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: config-position expression evaluation in the parser -- specifically, auth_source and body field names resolving a declaration reference rather than requiring a string literal. SUFFICIENT FOR deleting the duplicated literal and every claim that watches it. NOT satisfied by a claim that reads the literal through a lens, which would raise this to rung 2 and leave the duplicate writable.",

    "REVIEW TELL: an annotation naming a claim as what stops two things drifting apart. Read the claim and count the copies of the value. If the claim contains its own copy of the string, ask which edit it would survive.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "extdeps.google.drive", decl_name: "drive_token_env", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "extdeps.google.sheets", decl_name: "sheets_token_env", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "test.claim.review_sheet_transport_pin_witness", decl_name: "sheets_token_env_matches_the_configured_literal", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "test.claim.review_sheet_transport_pin_witness", decl_name: "drive_and_sheets_read_one_credential", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
